```rust
use itertools::Itertools;
fn main() {
    println!("{}", (10u8..).contains(&1));
    println!("{}", (&mut (10u8..)).contains(&1));
}
```

In debug mode, the above code prints `false` then panics with overflow. In release mode, it prints `false` then prints `true`.

This is because the first `contains()` call calls the inherent method on `RangeFrom`, while the second `contains()` call calls the `Itertools` method, which causes an integer overflow.

Deleting the `Itertools` import makes the code print `false` twice.

Adding the `contains` method to the standard library would have a similar effect to adding an `Itertools` import on all code. That is, stabilizing this method might silently change the run time behavior of code that calls `.contains()` on a `&mut RangeFrom`.

Should something be done about this?

Here is what I understand of this PR:

This changes `proc_macro::Ident::new("...", span)` to accept "$crate" as a valid argument instead of panicking. `proc_macro::Ident::new_raw("...", span)` will continue to not accept "$crate" because there is no such thing as raw `r#$crate`.

The identifier produced by `proc_macro::Ident::new("$crate", span)` will be analogous to the $crate identifier produced by `produce_dollar_crate!` and obtained by `receive_dollar_crate!` within the following macro_rules macros.

```rust
macro_rules! receive_dollar_crate {
    ($dollar_crate:ident) => {
        $dollar_crate::main
    };
}

macro_rules! produce_dollar_crate {
    () => {
        receive_dollar_crate!($crate)
    };
}

fn main() {
    let _ = produce_dollar_crate!();
}
```

When observed within the same crate (i.e. analogous in the macro_rules case to `receive_dollar_crate` and `produce_dollar_crate` being located in the same crate), `rustc -Zunpretty=expanded` will render the identifier as `crate`. But, this is just a rendering. This identifier does not match against a macro_rules matcher that literally expects `crate`. It can only be matched by `$ident:ident` or `$tt:tt`.

When observed within a different crate (i.e. `receive_dollar_crate` and `produce_dollar_crate` in different crates), the identifier is rendered as `::cratename` where cratename is the original name of the $crate's crate (disregarding any local rename). Again, this is just a rendering. It renders as what would ordinarily be multiple tokens but there is no other way to observe that this is anything different from a single identifier referring to the root module of the intended crate.

This API exposes 3 different usage patterns which were not previously expressible. Please consider adding tests.

### `proc_macro::Ident::new("$crate", Span::mixed_site())`

This identifier refers to the crate that defines the currently executing procedural macro. It can be used within the generated code for calling and/or re-exporting macros from that same crate.

Example:

```rust
#[proc_macro]
pub fn example(input: TokenStream) -> TokenStream {
    let krate = Ident::new("$crate", Span::mixed_site());
    quote! {
        pub use #krate::example;
        #krate::example! { ... }
    }
}
```

### `proc_macro::Ident::new("$crate", Span::call_site())`

This identifier refers to the crate that is currently being compiled. In generated code it is equivalent in almost all ways to `proc_macro::Ident::new("crate", Span::call_site())` (without $) with the following 2 exceptions:

1. The `$crate` identifier cannot be matched against a macro_rules matcher spelled `crate`, as mentioned already, whereas the `crate` identifier can.

2. When a proc macro expands to a macro_rules macro that expands to this identifier, and that macro_rules macro is called from a different crate than the one containing the generated macro_rules definition, the `$crate` identifier refers to the crate containing the macro_rules definition whereas the `crate` identifier refers to the crate containing the macro_rules caller.

Example:

```rust
#[proc_macro]
pub fn example(input: TokenStream) -> TokenStream {
    let krate = Ident::new("$crate", Span::call_site());
    quote! {
        mod m {
            // equivalent to crate::T, not equivalent to $crate::T (which is invalid syntax)
            pub type T = #krate::T;
            #[macro_export]
            macro_rules! example {
                ($n:ident) => {
                    // equivalent to $crate::T, not equivalent to crate::T
                    pub type $n = #krate::T;
                };
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### `proc_macro::Ident::new("$crate", some_input_token.span())`

This identifier refers to the crate that originated one of the input tokens that the currently executing procedural macro was given as input.

Example:

```rust
// crate A
B::example!();

// crate B
pub struct T;
#[macro_export]
macro_rules! example {
    () => {
        C::example!("")
    };
}

// crate C
#[proc_macro]
pub fn example(input: TokenStream) -> TokenStream {
    let tt = input.into_iter().next().unwrap();
    // B, not equivalent to call_site (which is A) nor mixed_site (which is C)
    let krate = Ident::new("$crate", tt.span());
    quote! {
        use #krate::T;
    }
}
```

### Alternatives

I would recommend considering whether this functionality should be exposed as an accessor on `Span` instead. Why is `Ident::new("$crate", Span::call_site())` a better spelling than `Span::call_site().krate()`? Is it possible that both should be supported?

```rust
impl Span {
    pub fn krate(&self) -> Ident;
}
```

### Future work

For referring to a crate that is _not_ the one containing the currently executing procedural macro, nor the crate currently being compiled, nor the crate that originated one of the macro input tokens, that remains unsolved and my preferred approach is https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/54363#issuecomment-1237544924.

We will eventually require an edition accessor on `Span` too, which gets the edition of the span's crate. I think it is fine for this to be a separate function on `Span` but one alternative I prefer less is:

```rust
impl Span {
    pub fn krate(&self) -> Crate;
}
impl Crate {
    pub fn ident(&self) -> Ident;
    pub fn edition(&self) -> Edition;
}
```

> It can only be matched by `$ident:ident` or `$tt:tt`.

Minor nit, `$crate` can also be matched by `$e:expr`,  `$e2:expr_2021`, `$m:meta`, `$p:pat`,  `$pp:pat_param`, `$pt:path` , `$s:stmt` , or`$t:ty`.

I will certainly add some tests to try to cover the new usage patterns, I just want to be clear that all of those usages can already be done today with `set_span` once you get your hands on a `$crate` token constructed by `proc-macro`'s parser, and so regardless of this PR should probably be tested (happy to add those tests to this PR since I'll already by adding the ones for `new`).

```rust
// crate A
pub struct TA;
B::example_b!();

// crate B
pub struct TB;
#[macro_export]
macro_rules! example_b {
    () => {
        C::example_c!($crate)
    };
}

// crate C
fn with_span(i: &Ident, span: Span) -> Ident {
  let mut i = i.clone();
  i.set_span(span);
  i
}

#[proc_macro]
pub fn example_c(input: TokenStream) -> TokenStream {
    let Some(TokenTree:Ident(krate_b)) = input.into_iter().next() else { panic!() };
    let krate_c = with_span(&krate_b, Span::mixed_site());
    let krate_a = with_span(&krate_b, Span::call_site());
    quote! {
        use #krate_a::TA as _;
        use #krate_b::TB as _;
        use #krate_c::example_c as _; // can't export a TC from a proc-macro
    }
}
```

This PR is mostly to fix the round-trip `Display` issue (and also to align the behavior of `proc-macro::Ident` with `ident` in `macro_rules`). Solving the macro-dependency-reference problem is a waaaaay too big for a 2-line bug fix. The linked approach for solving that seems great, by the way, 100% support.

