We could ask, comparatively, I suppose, whether we buy the safety argument here,

```rust
#[cfg(target_arch = "x86_64")]
#[unsafe(no_mangle)]
pub extern "sysv64" fn f() {
    /// Returns an integer guaranteed to be evenly divisible by 16.
    ///
    // SAFETY: This just returns an address.
    #[unsafe(naked)]
    extern "sysv64" fn get_a_cfa() -> u64 {
        core::arch::naked_asm!("lea rax, [rsp + 8]", "ret");
    }
    if get_a_cfa() % 16 != 0 {
        // SAFETY: The `sysv64` ABI guarantees that a caller must
        // align the canonical frame address (CFA) to a 16-byte
        // boundary.  After doing this, it will push the 8-byte return
        // address, so on function entry `rsp + 8` will be the CFA and
        // will be 16-byte aligned.
        //
        // The language guarantees that, given the syntactic forms
        // we've used, this program will be interpreted such that
        // the call to `get_a_cfa` will follow the rules of this ABI.
        //
        // Therefore, the `rsp + 8` address returned by `get_a_cfa`
        // will be evenly divisible by 16 and this branch will never
        // be taken.
        unsafe { core::hint::unreachable_unchecked() };
    }
}
```

and whether the behaviors being relied upon in this safety analysis represent guarantees of the language.

Similar in spirit, and along the lines of the hypothetical that @scottmcm raised, but obviously missing a guarantee as noted:

```rust
#[cfg(target_arch = "x86_64")]
#[unsafe(no_mangle)]
pub extern "sysv64" fn f(_: u128, _: u128, _: u128, x: u8) {
    if &raw const x as usize % 16 != 0 {
        // (UN)SAFETY: The `sysv64` ABI guarantees that a caller must
        // align the canonical frame address (CFA) to a 16-byte
        // boundary.  It also guarantees that the value of `x` must be
        // passed on the stack at exactly the address of the CFA (due
        // to the first three arguments consuming all of the registers
        // used to pass arguments).
        //
        // We're missing, however, a guarantee from the language that
        // it hasn't chosen to make a copy of `x` somewhere below the
        // CFA, and to point the binding of `x` to that instead.  It'd
        // be surprising for it to do it, but it's the guarantee we're
        // missing.
        unsafe { core::hint::unreachable_unchecked() };
    }
}
```

